---
import ContentPage from "../layouts/ContentPage.astro";
import Callout from "../components/Callout.astro";
import Chips from "../components/Chips.astro";
---
<ContentPage
  title="Colophon"
  active="colophon"
  kicker="Colophon"
  headline="How this site is built."
  stand="The publish boundary, practicing what it documents."
>
  <p>
    This site is the third boundary in the lab's three-boundary model: it describes the
    infrastructure for anyone to read, without exposing anything the other two boundaries
    guard. Today that property comes from the bluntest mechanism available — the lab's
    data is not sent here, so there is nothing to expose.
  </p>

  <h2>Built by hand, on purpose</h2>

  <p>
    The pages you are reading are hand-authored Astro components. So is the physical layer
    of the <a href="/explorer">interactive explorer</a>: a curated graph of generic aliases
    that shows the architecture's shape without naming a single real host, address, or
    identifier. It does not originate in <code>inventory.yaml</code>, and
    <code>src/data/graph.ts</code> says so in its opening comment.
  </p>

  <Callout tone="publish" label="What is not here yet">
    <p>
      The <a href="/publish">Publish</a> boundary is designed around two mechanisms — an
      allowlist projection that emits only fields explicitly marked publishable, and an
      independent tripwire that scans build output for anything secret-shaped and fails the
      build on a match. <strong>Neither is built.</strong> No inventory field carries a
      publication marker and this repository's build runs no output scan. They are the
      prerequisite for ever feeding this site real lab data, not a description of what
      guards it today.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <p>
    The public build never receives <code>inventory.yaml</code>, directly or indirectly.
    There is no code path in this site that reads the source file, and no generated
    artifact from the Describe boundary is checked in here.
  </p>

  <h2>The stack</h2>

  <p>
    The architecture is deliberately minimal. Astro 7 compiles the site to static HTML
    at build time: no server process, no server-side rendering, no API routes. The pages
    you are reading are Astro components — hand-authored prose, not generated output.
  </p>

  <p>
    The one interactive piece is the infrastructure explorer, a React Flow island
    client-hydrated from a graph module compiled into the bundle. The island runs entirely
    in the browser and makes no requests to any lab system. It is interactive in the UI
    sense; it is inert in the network sense.
  </p>

  <Chips items={["Astro 7", "React Flow", "hand-authored graph", "static HTML"]} />

  <h2>The design system</h2>

  <p>
    Three typefaces carry the visual hierarchy. <strong>Instrument Serif</strong> handles
    the display layer: page headlines, pull quotes, and the stand lines that open each
    section. It is a high-contrast transitional serif that reads well at large sizes on
    dark backgrounds. <strong>DM Sans</strong> handles body text and UI chrome — a
    geometric grotesque that stays legible at small sizes without feeling clinical.
    <strong>DM Mono</strong> renders labels, chips, code fragments, and nav group
    headings: the data layer, where monospace signals precision.
  </p>

  <p>
    The color story is copper on warm charcoal. Copper is used sparingly: active nav
    states, accent borders on callouts, inline links, and numbers in stat displays.
    Everything else recedes into a warm gray scale. The palette is dark by default,
    not as a stylistic gesture but because the subject matter is infrastructure running
    in a rack — not a product landing page in morning light.
  </p>

  <h2>What this site is not</h2>

  <p>
    The site carries no live data. Nothing it displays changes after the build runs.
    There is no polling loop, no webhook listener, no cron job refreshing a feed from the
    lab. The explorer's graph is a curated representation; if the allowlist generator is
    ever built, its physical layer could be derived from the same inventory as everything
    else — and the tripwire would have to be built in the same breath.
  </p>

  <p>
    No path runs from this site back into the lab. There is no outbound API call, no
    server-side handler, no form that submits to a lab system. The boundary between
    Publish and the two boundaries behind it is strictly one-directional: data flows out
    at build time, and that is the only crossing it makes.
  </p>

  <p>
    The site cannot leak the lab's secrets. They were never compiled into it — and while
    that remains true by absence rather than by filter, it is worth saying plainly rather
    than dressing it up as a pipeline.
  </p>
</ContentPage>
